s when the granularity becomes 1 (i.e., we have reduced to strings of length 1? No, the granularity is chunk size; stopping at 1 means we stop when we attempt to remove chunks of size 1? Possibly we stop the algorithm when we have completed a pass with no removals at granularity 2? Hmm.\n\nLet's re-read: \"Start chunk removal at granularity two. Try chunks from left to right and accept removal only if required failure items remain. Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity. Double granularity when no removal succeeds and stop at singleton granularity.\"\n\nAlternate interpretation: We have a single reduction process. We start with granularity = 2. We go through the list from left to right, attempting to remove chunks of that size. If a removal succeeds (i.e., required_failure_items still present), we actually remove it, and then we increase granularity by 1 (coarser) and restart scanning from the beginning of the new list. If we finish a full pass with no removals, we then double the granularity (so now 4) and try again, but if next pass also no removals, double again (8), etc. Stop when the granularity becomes 1? But starting at 2, doubling gives even numbers. So \"singleton granularity\" might refer to when the chunk size equals the number of items left? Or maybe \"singleton\" means size = 1 after we double? Actually \"singleton\" could mean the list is reduced to a single item? Not clear.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the algorithm is: we have a list, we want to reduce it by repeatedly removing a \"chunk\" (a contiguous block) of size equal to \"granularity\". We start at granularity 2. For each possible chunk from left to right, we test removal. If removal leaves required items, we commit that removal and then increase granularity by 1 (restart). If no chunk works, we double granularity and try again. Once we reach a granularity that is greater than the current list length? Stop when granularity becomes 1? Actually granularities: 2, then if