single chunk of any size (?) can be removed while still preserving required_failure_items. But the algorithm might guarantee that. However, we need to compute it explicitly.\n\nGiven the ambiguity, we must deduce the intended algorithm from the description. Possibly it's the classic \"ddmin\" algorithm from delta debugging? The description resembles delta debugging but with chunk sizes doubling and restarting after success. In delta debugging, we start with a granularity of 2, try to remove each subset (in order), if removal succeeds, we restart with the reduced set; if fails, we continue; when all subsets of a given size fail, we increase granularity. The standard ddmin uses power of 2 progression. But here \"restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity\" and \"double granularity when no removal succeeds\". That matches the ddmin algorithm exactly: start with granularity=2, for each chunk of that size (in order), test removal; if any removal succeeds, set granularity = max(2, previous granularity)?? Actually in ddmin, after a successful removal, you restart with the reduced set and the same granularity? No, delta debugging algorithm: start with n=2; divide set into n subsets; test subset; if test fails, try complement; if complement fails, increase n=2n. Etc. But the description here: \"Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity.\" That suggests after a success, you increase granularity (make it coarser, i.e., larger chunk size). That is opposite of typical: after success, you want to try finer granularity? Actually typical ddmin: after you find a failing subset, you then try to reduce it further by halving it. But here they say \"coarser\", meaning larger chunks. That seems like after removal, you want to try to remove larger chunks next? Hmm.\n\nMaybe it's a variant: Start with chunk size 2. For each chunk, if removing it is OK, then remove it and then try a coarser granularity (bigger chunks) on the new list. This 