perty holds). If yes, removal is accepted.\n\nWe start with granularity = 2. We attempt to remove chunks of size exactly granularity? Or up to granularity? \"Try chunks from left to right\" suggests we consider all chunks of that exact size? Could also be overlapping, but typical is non-overlapping? The description says \"Try chunks from left to right\" – likely overlapping sliding windows of size granularity. For example, with list [a,b,c,d,e] and granularity 2, we consider [a,b], then [b,c], then [c,d], then [d,e] from left to right. For each, we test removal. The order is left to right, and positions are based on original indices before any removal. But after a removal, the list changes, so we restart scanning.\n\nNow, \"Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity.\" So after we remove a chunk (say chunk at indices i to i+granularity-1), we have a new shorter list. Then we set new granularity = previous granularity + 1 (coarser means larger size). Then we start over from the beginning of the new list with that new granularity. If no removal is possible at the current granularity (i.e., scanning all chunks from left to right, none preserves property), then we double the granularity (multiply by 2) and try again. \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds and stop at singleton granularity.\" What does \"singleton granularity\" mean? It could mean when the granularity becomes 1. But if we start at 2 and double on failure, we get 4,8,... never 1. Unless we also have a case where after success we increment by 1, so we might get to 3,4,5,... and then after a failure we double? That could lead to odd/even. For example, start 2. If success, go to 3. If no success at 3, double to 6. If success at 6, go to 7, etc. Eventually we might hit a granularity that equals current list length or greater. The phrase \"stop at singleton granularity\" might mean stop when the granularity becomes 1? But we never have 1 unless we start at 2 and after success increment to 3, etc. Not 1. Maybe \"singleton\" means when the list is reduced to a single element (i.e., only one item). But that's not granularity. Alternatively, the algorithm might be a variant where the granularity is the number of subsets (not chunk size). In delta debugging, n is number of subsets. Starting with n=2, try each subset; if a subset fails, increase n = min(2n, len(list)). That yields n=2,4,8,... until n >= len(list). Then you stop. That is typical. But here they mention chunk removal and granularity, not subsets. \"Chunk removal\" suggests removing contiguous blocks. However, delta debugging also uses contiguous subsets (chunks). In ddmin, you start by partitioning the list into 2 chunks (first half, second half). You test each chunk for removal (i.e., removing that chunk). If removing a chunk fails (i.e., property fails), you revert. If it succeeds, you reduce to that chunk and continue with the same granularity? Actually ddmin algorithm: if set fails, split into two subsets; test each subset; if a subset fails, recurse on that subset; else test complements and increase split count. The description here is different.\n\nGiven the ambiguity and the fact that the problem is from a coding challenge, I think the expected algorithm is a simplified version: \n- Let L = list of items.\n- Let g = 2.\n- Let attempts = 0.\n- While True:\n    - removed_any = False\n    - For each starting index i from 0 to len(L)-g (inclusive):\n        - Consider chunk = L[i:i+g]\n        - If removing chunk (i.e., L' = L[:i] + L[i+g:]) still contains all required_failure_items (check that each required item appears at least once in L'):\n            - Remove it: L = L'\n            - attempts += 1 (count this successful attempt)\n            - g = g + 1 (coarser)\n            - removed_any = True\n            - break (restart scanning from beginning with new g)\n    - If not removed_any:\n        - attempts += number of chunks tried? Or count each attempt? The schema has \"attempts\" integer. It likely counts each time we consider a chunk for removal (both successful and unsuccessful). So inside the for loop, for each chunk we consider, we increment attempts regardless of outcome. Then after a full pass with no removals, we double g: g = g * 2.\n        - If g > len(L) or g == 1? Probably stop when g > len(L) (can't have chunks larger than list). But also \"stop at singleton granularity\" could mean stop when g becomes 1? But we double so it becomes larger. Alternatively, stop when g becomes 1 after doubling? No.\n        - Perhaps we stop when g = 1? That would imply that after we have done a pass with no removals at granularity >1, we set g = 1 and then try removals of single elements? That could be the final step to check one-minimality. The phrase \"stop at singleton granularity\" could mean we stop after we have attempted removal at granularity 1 (singleton) and no removal succeeds. Then the list is 1-minimal. The algorithm might be: start g=2. While g >= 1: try chunks of size g. If removal, increase g by 1. If no removal, decrease g? But they say double on failure, not decrease.\n\nI think I need to look at typical \"chunk reduction\" in test case minimization. There's a known algorithm \"ddmin\" with variations. However, given the specific output fields, the \"one_minimal\" flag is likely computed by checking if removing any single item (not chunk) fails. That is a common post-reduction check.\n\nLet's attempt to implement a plausible algorithm and see if it matches the expected outputs for the given fixture cases? We don't have expected outputs, but we can maybe infer from the names: The case IDs a-000, a-001, a-002 have different required_failure_items. The items list is the same. The algorithm should produce different reduced_items. For example, for required_failure_items = [\"t0\",\"t9\"], we likely want to keep t0 and t9 and maybe some others to maintain contiguity? But we can remove any chunk as long as t0 and t9 remain.