ity=1? Or \"singleton\" could refer to the list length being reduced to a single item. But \"stop at singleton granularity\" could be interpreted as: when no removal succeeds, and the current granularity is 1 (singleton), stop the process. But we never get to 1 via doubling. So perhaps the algorithm includes a step: after doubling, if the new granularity becomes 1 (i.e., from 2 to 1? impossible), or maybe after doubling, if result > len(list), then set granularity = len(list) and then eventually reduce granularity to 1? That seems complicated.\n\nAlternative: \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds and stop at singleton granularity\" could mean: when no removal succeeds, you double the granularity only if the current granularity is not already singleton (i.e., >1). Once granularity becomes 1, you stop. So the process: start g=2. While g > 1: try removals; if success, g=g+1, restart; if full pass no removal, if g>1, then g=g*2; if g becomes 1 after doubling? Not possible. So maybe there's a step where after doubling, if g > len(list), set g = len(list) or maybe 1? Unclear.\n\nAnother viewpoint: The algorithm might be the \"ddmin\" algorithm for test case minimization, which is well-documented. The description matches the high-level idea. In ddmin, you start with a set and divide into 2 subsets, test each subset for failure; if a subset fails, you reduce to that subset and continue with 2 subsets again. If no subset fails, you increase the number of subsets to 4, etc. That is: start n=2, partition into n subsets, test each subset; if a subset fails (i.e., removing it makes property fail, so the subset itself is failing), then you continue with that subset (which is smaller) and reset n=2. If no subset fails, double n and retry. Stop when n >= size of current set. That yields a 1-minimal set. The \"granularity\" here is the number of subsets, not chunk size. And \"chunks from left to right\" refers to the subsets (which are contiguous). \"Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity\" could mean after a successful removal (i.e., a subset fails), you restart with the same granularity? Actually in ddmin, after a subset fails, you reduce to that subset and restart with n=2 (finer granularity). But here they say \"slightly coarser granularity\", which is opposite. So maybe it's a different variant.\n\nGiven the confusion, I'll look at the output schema: It requires \"one_minimal\" boolean. In ddmin, the final result is guaranteed to be 1-minimal. So that fits. \"attempts\" counts the number of property tests (removal attempts). In ddmin, each test of a subset or complement counts as an attempt. That seems plausible.\n\nBut the description says \"Try chunks from left to right\" which matches testing subsets in order. \"Accept removal only if required failure items remain\" means we test if removing the chunk (i.e., the complement) leaves the property? In delta debugging, we test the subset itself for failure. But here they talk about \"removal\" of chunk, and property is \"required failure items remain\". So the property holds if all required items are present. Removing a chunk might remove some required items, causing property to fail. We want to remove chunks that do NOT cause failure (i.e., the removed chunk does not contain required items). So we are trying to remove chunks that are not essential. That is like minimizing by removing non-essential parts. In ddmin, you test subsets (chunks) to see if they cause failure. But here we test removal. So it's symmetric: if removing chunk leaves property, then that chunk is unnecessary. So we only accept removal if it's safe. That matches ddmin's test of complement. In ddmin, you test if a subset (the complement of the chunk) fails. Actually standard ddmin: if the subset (the chunk) fails, then you can reduce to that subset. But here we test if removing chunk (i.e., the complement) still has property. That is equivalent to testing if the chunk itself does NOT contain all required items. So the algorithm is slightly different.\n\nGiven the uncertainty, I think the safest is to implement a straightforward interpretation that matches the literal words.\n\nLet's define:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    items = case['items'][:]  # copy\n    required = set(case['required_failure_items'])  # use set for efficient check\n    # Note: required_failure_items may have duplicates? Probably not, but we assume unique.\n\n    g = 2\n    attempts = 0\n    reduced = items[:]\n\n    while True:\n        # If g > len(reduced): cannot remove chunks of size g, so we might stop? But if g > len(reduced), we can't have any chunk. So perhaps we should set g = len(reduced) or stop.\n        # Also \"stop at singleton granularity\" might mean stop when g == 1? But we never get g=1.\n        # Let's assume we stop when g > len(reduced) or g == 1.\n        if g > len(reduced) or g == 1:\n            break\n\n        removed_any = False\n        # Try all chunks of size g from left to right\n        for i in range(len(reduced) - g + 1):\n            attempts += 1  # count this trial\n            new_list = reduced[:i] + reduced[i+g:]\n            if all(r in new_list for r in required):\n                # removal accepted\n                reduced = new_list\n                g = g + 1  # slightly coarser\n                removed_any = True\n                break  # restart scanning\n\n        if not removed_any:\n            # no removal succeeded\n            g = g * 2  # double granularity\n            # if g becomes too large, maybe set to len(reduced) or 1?\n            # According to \"stop at singleton granularity\", maybe when g >= len(reduced), we set g = 1?\n            if g >= len(reduced):\n                g = len(reduced)  # or 1? But let's set to 1 to stop next iteration.\n                # Actually if g >= len, we cannot have chunks. So we should stop.\n                # But \"stop at singleton granularity\" suggests we want to try granularity 1.\n                # So maybe we set g = 1 and continue? But then we would loop again with g=1 and try singleton removals.\n                break  # for now just break to avoid infinite loop. Need to decide.\n\n    # After loop, we have reduced list. Check one-minimal: try removing each single item and see if property holds.\n    one_minimal = True\n    for i in range(len(reduced)):\n        test = reduced[:i] + reduced[i+1:]\n        if all(r in test for r in required):\n            one_minimal = False\n            break\n\n    return {\n        \"attempts\": attempts,\n        \"one_minimal\": one_minimal,\n        \"reduced_items\": reduced\n    }\n\nBut we need to refine the loop termination condition per description. The phrase \"stop at singleton granularity\" might mean we stop the process when we have attempted removal at granularity 1 (i.e., single element chunks) and no removal succeeded. So we should include a pass with g=1. How to get to g=1? From description: we double when no removal succeeds. Starting at 2, after a failure, we double to 4, then 8, etc. We never get to 1. So maybe after a full pass with no removal at some granularity, we set granularity to 1 directly (i.e., we \"stop at singleton granularity\" meaning we then try singleton removal). But the phrase \"double granularity when no removal succeeds\" might only apply until we reach a certain point, and then we set granularity to 1. Alternatively, maybe \"singleton granularity\" means granularity = 1, and we stop after that pass. So the algorithm could be: start g=2. While g >= 1: try chunks of size g. If success, g = g+1, restart. If no success, if g > 1, then g = g*2, else (if g==1) break. That would work if we allow g to become 1 via doubling? Not possible. So maybe we need to incorporate g = 1 after a failure at g=2? That doesn't match \"double\".\n\nAnother possible interpretation: The \"granularity\" is the number of chunks we divide the list into, not the chunk size. And \"singleton gran